1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,561
Imagine a small grey room.

2
00:00:01,627 --> 00:00:05,589
A federal agent slides a recorder across a
steel table and presses

3
00:00:05,655 --> 00:00:07,756
play. And the voice that comes out,

4
00:00:07,823 --> 00:00:11,724
calm, certain, talking about a stock days
before the news breaks,

5
00:00:11,791 --> 00:00:14,072
is yours. There is no arguing with it.

6
00:00:14,138 --> 00:00:15,999
There is no explaining it away.

7
00:00:16,066 --> 00:00:18,767
It is simply you, on tape, in your own
words.

8
00:00:18,833 --> 00:00:22,735
This film is the story of the first time
the untouchables of Wall

9
00:00:22,801 --> 00:00:24,242
Street heard that sound.

10
00:00:25,292 --> 00:00:29,517
For a long time, almost everyone on Wall
Street believed the same

11
00:00:29,584 --> 00:00:31,794
quiet thing. That at the very top,

12
00:00:31,861 --> 00:00:33,161
the game was rigged.

13
00:00:33,228 --> 00:00:37,388
That a small handful of people always
seemed to know — about the

14
00:00:37,454 --> 00:00:40,640
merger, the bad quarter, the secret deal —
a day,

15
00:00:40,706 --> 00:00:43,372
or an hour, before the rest of the world.

16
00:00:43,438 --> 00:00:46,363
You could see it in the timing of the
trades.

17
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:48,380
You could feel it in your gut.

18
00:00:48,447 --> 00:00:50,787
You just could never, ever prove it.

19
00:00:51,837 --> 00:00:55,900
The man at the center of this story ran a
hedge fund called Galleon.

20
00:00:55,967 --> 00:00:59,015
At its peak it managed about seven billion
dollars.

21
00:00:59,081 --> 00:01:00,336
He was a billionaire.

22
00:01:00,403 --> 00:01:03,510
He was charming, brilliant, generous to
his friends,

23
00:01:03,577 --> 00:01:05,728
and by every measure the world uses,

24
00:01:05,795 --> 00:01:06,930
he was untouchable.

25
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:10,640
Here is what you need to know before we
begin.

26
00:01:10,707 --> 00:01:12,731
He was not caught by a paper trail,

27
00:01:12,798 --> 00:01:15,284
or a clever accountant, or a jealous
rival.

28
00:01:15,351 --> 00:01:18,936
He was caught the way the government
catches mobsters and drug

29
00:01:19,003 --> 00:01:20,333
cartels. They listened.

30
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:21,788
They recorded his phone.

31
00:01:21,854 --> 00:01:24,977
Then, in a courtroom, they played him back
to himself.

32
00:01:26,027 --> 00:01:29,632
So the question of this film is not
whether the rich cheat.

33
00:01:29,698 --> 00:01:31,714
You already suspect that they do.

34
00:01:31,781 --> 00:01:35,202
The real question is harder, and it is
more interesting.

35
00:01:35,269 --> 00:01:38,201
What does it actually take to catch one of
them?

36
00:01:38,268 --> 00:01:41,384
And what did the people chasing him have
to become,

37
00:01:41,451 --> 00:01:42,978
to finally make it stick?

38
00:01:44,028 --> 00:01:46,998
Now hold one more image next to the
billionaire.

39
00:01:47,065 --> 00:01:51,087
On the other side of his trades sits
someone completely ordinary.

40
00:01:51,154 --> 00:01:52,268
A retired teacher.

41
00:01:52,334 --> 00:01:54,995
A nurse moving her savings into the
market.

42
00:01:55,062 --> 00:01:59,394
A young couple buying their first shares
of a company they believe in.

43
00:01:59,460 --> 00:02:02,431
They are playing what they think is a fair
game,

44
00:02:02,497 --> 00:02:05,467
and they have no idea that the game is not
fair.

45
00:02:05,534 --> 00:02:08,752
That is the real injury at the center of
this story.

46
00:02:08,819 --> 00:02:11,108
Not a vault broken open in the night,

47
00:02:11,175 --> 00:02:13,526
but a market tilted in broad daylight,

48
00:02:13,593 --> 00:02:17,430
one secret at a time, against every single
person who actually

49
00:02:17,496 --> 00:02:18,672
followed the rules.

50
00:02:19,722 --> 00:02:23,351
To understand why this case shook the
entire industry,

51
00:02:23,418 --> 00:02:25,569
you have to understand one word.

52
00:02:25,635 --> 00:02:27,719
The word runs the whole street.

53
00:02:27,786 --> 00:02:32,490
Edge. On Wall Street, an edge is the thing
you know that the person on

54
00:02:32,557 --> 00:02:35,044
the other side of the trade does not.

55
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:37,059
Better research. Faster math.

56
00:02:37,126 --> 00:02:39,008
A sharper read of the crowd.

57
00:02:39,075 --> 00:02:42,435
A legal edge is the engine of the entire
business.

58
00:02:42,502 --> 00:02:46,333
Every fund, every desk, every analyst is
hunting for one,

59
00:02:46,399 --> 00:02:48,886
every single hour the market is open.

60
00:02:50,786 --> 00:02:54,140
Raj Rajaratnam was very, very good at the
legal kind.

61
00:02:54,207 --> 00:02:56,168
He was relentless on the phone.

62
00:02:56,235 --> 00:03:00,348
He built a vast web of contacts across the
technology and finance

63
00:03:00,415 --> 00:03:02,693
worlds, and he worked it constantly,

64
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,734
trading gossip, favors, and reads on
companies.

65
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:09,850
He turned Galleon into one of the most
watched funds of its era,

66
00:03:09,917 --> 00:03:14,093
and the returns made him rich and made the
people around him rich.

67
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:17,071
Proximity to him felt like proximity to
money.

68
00:03:18,121 --> 00:03:21,786
To the people who worked for him,
Rajaratnam could seem like the

69
00:03:21,853 --> 00:03:23,284
best-connected man alive.

70
00:03:23,351 --> 00:03:26,615
He ran the fund like a newsroom that never
went to sleep,

71
00:03:26,681 --> 00:03:28,800
pulling in fragments from everywhere,

72
00:03:28,867 --> 00:03:31,501
rewarding whoever brought him something
sharp,

73
00:03:31,568 --> 00:03:34,889
and never quite forgetting whoever showed
up empty-handed.

74
00:03:34,956 --> 00:03:38,048
Inside that world, knowing things was the
entire game.

75
00:03:38,114 --> 00:03:40,519
The earlier you knew, the bigger you were.

76
00:03:40,586 --> 00:03:43,965
Most of what reached him was ordinary and
completely legal,

77
00:03:44,031 --> 00:03:45,921
the daily weather of a busy fund.

78
00:03:45,988 --> 00:03:48,679
This story is only about the part that was
not.

79
00:03:49,729 --> 00:03:51,786
But there is a second kind of edge,

80
00:03:51,853 --> 00:03:55,261
and it does not come from working harder
than anyone else.

81
00:03:55,328 --> 00:03:56,974
It comes from knowing first.

82
00:03:57,040 --> 00:03:59,803
Knowing the earnings before they are
announced.

83
00:03:59,869 --> 00:04:02,279
Knowing the takeover before it is signed.

84
00:04:02,346 --> 00:04:06,107
Knowing the bad news while everyone else
is still being told the

85
00:04:06,174 --> 00:04:09,171
company is fine. That kind of edge is not
research.

86
00:04:09,238 --> 00:04:12,646
It is theft: information that belongs to a
company and its

87
00:04:12,713 --> 00:04:15,828
shareholders, not to a trader with the
right friends.

88
00:04:16,878 --> 00:04:19,388
And that second edge has one beautiful,

89
00:04:19,455 --> 00:04:22,545
terrible feature. It almost never leaves a
mark.

90
00:04:22,612 --> 00:04:25,637
A normal crime gives you something to
point at.

91
00:04:25,704 --> 00:04:27,249
A body. A broken window.

92
00:04:27,315 --> 00:04:31,371
A missing car. This crime is a single
sentence whispered into a

93
00:04:31,437 --> 00:04:34,077
telephone. A number, traded for a number.

94
00:04:34,143 --> 00:04:36,589
And then a trade that looks, on paper,

95
00:04:36,656 --> 00:04:41,098
exactly like a smart guess by a man
already famous for smart guesses.

96
00:04:41,164 --> 00:04:45,284
So ask yourself the question the
government could not answer for

97
00:04:45,351 --> 00:04:47,668
decades. How do you prove a whisper?

98
00:04:48,718 --> 00:04:51,003
For most of the history of the market,

99
00:04:51,070 --> 00:04:53,896
the honest answer was that you mostly
couldn't.

100
00:04:53,963 --> 00:04:56,489
Regulators could see the suspicious trade.

101
00:04:56,555 --> 00:04:59,141
They could see that someone bought
heavily,

102
00:04:59,208 --> 00:05:02,696
right before the good news, and sold right
before the bad.

103
00:05:02,763 --> 00:05:06,912
But seeing a pattern is not the same as
proving that a specific human

104
00:05:06,979 --> 00:05:08,723
being knew a specific secret.

105
00:05:08,789 --> 00:05:12,939
A suspicious chart is a hunch, and you
cannot send a person to prison

106
00:05:13,006 --> 00:05:17,215
on a hunch. A jury needs the moment the
secret actually changed hands,

107
00:05:17,282 --> 00:05:19,507
and that moment was always invisible.

108
00:05:20,557 --> 00:05:22,624
Sit with what that meant for a second,

109
00:05:22,691 --> 00:05:25,519
because it is the quiet engine of the
whole problem.

110
00:05:25,586 --> 00:05:29,393
It meant the people most able to cheat
were also the people hardest to

111
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:32,125
catch. The bigger and more powerful your
network,

112
00:05:32,192 --> 00:05:34,748
the more deniable any single phone call
became.

113
00:05:34,815 --> 00:05:36,773
You could always say it was a guess.

114
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:38,852
You could always say it was research,

115
00:05:38,919 --> 00:05:41,910
a mosaic of public facts assembled by a
brilliant mind.

116
00:05:41,977 --> 00:05:43,391
The edge protected itself.

117
00:05:43,458 --> 00:05:45,960
The better you were at it, the safer you
were.

118
00:05:47,010 --> 00:05:49,854
So a culture grew up inside that blind
spot.

119
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:52,828
Not everyone — most of Wall Street is
honest,

120
00:05:52,895 --> 00:05:55,028
and most edges really are earned.

121
00:05:55,094 --> 00:05:59,295
But at the very top, among a small set of
people who all knew one

122
00:05:59,362 --> 00:06:01,753
another, a different habit took hold.

123
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:04,276
Information became a kind of currency.

124
00:06:04,342 --> 00:06:06,927
You gave a secret, and you got one back.

125
00:06:06,994 --> 00:06:10,678
Favors, with a price measured not in
dollars but in tips,

126
00:06:10,744 --> 00:06:12,877
and the tips were worth millions.

127
00:06:13,927 --> 00:06:17,038
And the currency moved through ordinary
friendship,

128
00:06:17,105 --> 00:06:19,362
which is what made it so hard to see.

129
00:06:19,428 --> 00:06:22,661
A college classmate who now sat on a
corporate board.

130
00:06:22,728 --> 00:06:26,693
A consultant who advised everyone and
therefore heard everything.

131
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:30,969
An executive, flattered to be asked his
opinion by a famous investor.

132
00:06:31,035 --> 00:06:33,109
None of them, on a normal Tuesday,

133
00:06:33,176 --> 00:06:35,128
looked anything like a criminal.

134
00:06:35,195 --> 00:06:37,147
They looked like a contact list.

135
00:06:37,213 --> 00:06:39,226
That was the genius of the thing.

136
00:06:39,293 --> 00:06:41,001
That was also the rot of it.

137
00:06:42,051 --> 00:06:45,253
Rajaratnam sat in the middle of a web
exactly like that.

138
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:49,265
The government would later argue that it
ran from inside a giant chip

139
00:06:49,332 --> 00:06:53,220
company in the West, through one of the
most famous consulting firms

140
00:06:53,286 --> 00:06:57,117
in the world, and all the way into the
boardroom of one of the most

141
00:06:57,184 --> 00:06:58,556
powerful banks on earth.

142
00:06:58,623 --> 00:07:00,567
Secrets came in along those lines.

143
00:07:00,634 --> 00:07:04,293
Trades went out. And the fund kept beating
the market just often

144
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:08,248
enough, and just quietly enough, to look
like pure genius instead of

145
00:07:08,314 --> 00:07:09,314
something else.

146
00:07:10,222 --> 00:07:14,161
For years, that was as close to a perfect
crime as money can buy.

147
00:07:14,228 --> 00:07:16,591
It was quiet. It was wildly profitable,

148
00:07:16,658 --> 00:07:19,142
and it was deniable at every single step.

149
00:07:19,209 --> 00:07:23,330
It was the kind of thing that everyone in
the business suspected was

150
00:07:23,396 --> 00:07:27,275
happening, and that no prosecutor on earth
could actually touch.

151
00:07:27,342 --> 00:07:29,584
If the story had simply stayed there,

152
00:07:29,650 --> 00:07:33,953
in that comfortable shadow, you would
never have heard this man's name.

153
00:07:35,003 --> 00:07:39,177
This matters to you even if you have never
bought a single share in

154
00:07:39,244 --> 00:07:41,486
your life. Your pension owns stocks.

155
00:07:41,553 --> 00:07:43,796
Your retirement account owns stocks.

156
00:07:43,863 --> 00:07:47,912
The savings of almost everyone you know
are quietly riding on one

157
00:07:47,979 --> 00:07:50,533
promise. That the market is roughly fair.

158
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:53,715
That nobody at the table can simply see
the cards.

159
00:07:53,781 --> 00:07:56,273
When a handful of insiders can see them,

160
00:07:56,340 --> 00:07:58,334
the market stops being a market.

161
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:01,453
It becomes a private club with a public
entrance,

162
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:04,572
and the rest of us are standing in that
entrance,

163
00:08:04,639 --> 00:08:08,626
paying full price for a seat in a game
that was already decided.

164
00:08:09,676 --> 00:08:11,861
But somewhere inside the government,

165
00:08:11,928 --> 00:08:14,780
a small group of investigators got tired
of it.

166
00:08:14,847 --> 00:08:17,214
Tired of watching the pattern light up,

167
00:08:17,281 --> 00:08:21,408
again and again, and never being able to
reach the people behind it.

168
00:08:21,474 --> 00:08:22,688
They had the trades.

169
00:08:22,755 --> 00:08:23,969
They had the timing.

170
00:08:24,035 --> 00:08:25,431
They had the suspicion.

171
00:08:25,498 --> 00:08:29,747
What they did not have was the one thing a
jury believes more than any

172
00:08:29,813 --> 00:08:32,848
chart, more than any expert, more than any
theory.

173
00:08:32,915 --> 00:08:34,614
They did not have the words.

174
00:08:35,664 --> 00:08:37,663
So they decided to go and get them.

175
00:08:37,730 --> 00:08:40,186
And to do it, they reached for a tool
that,

176
00:08:40,252 --> 00:08:44,079
until that moment, no one in the country
had ever really aimed at a

177
00:08:44,146 --> 00:08:45,231
man in a good suit.

178
00:08:46,281 --> 00:08:48,875
To see how the secrets actually traveled,

179
00:08:48,942 --> 00:08:50,144
follow four people.

180
00:08:50,211 --> 00:08:52,109
None of them ran a hedge fund.

181
00:08:52,176 --> 00:08:56,352
Each of them simply sat somewhere a fund
manager can only dream of

182
00:08:56,418 --> 00:08:59,266
sitting: inside the company, before the
news.

183
00:08:59,332 --> 00:09:02,306
Each was charged separately, in their own
case,

184
00:09:02,373 --> 00:09:04,904
with their own story and their own fate.

185
00:09:04,971 --> 00:09:07,122
But the lines they sat on all ran,

186
00:09:07,189 --> 00:09:09,593
eventually, toward the same telephone.

187
00:09:10,643 --> 00:09:14,461
The first sat inside Intel, one of the
largest chipmakers on earth.

188
00:09:14,528 --> 00:09:18,289
A man named Rajiv Goel, a friend of
Rajaratnam's going back years.

189
00:09:18,356 --> 00:09:22,174
From a seat like that, you can see a
quarter before the public can.

190
00:09:22,241 --> 00:09:26,230
You can know whether the numbers are going
to be good or bad while the

191
00:09:26,297 --> 00:09:28,406
rest of the market is still guessing,

192
00:09:28,472 --> 00:09:30,752
still hoping, still trading in the dark.

193
00:09:32,652 --> 00:09:36,830
The second sat at McKinsey, the consulting
firm that quietly advises

194
00:09:36,897 --> 00:09:38,986
the giants of the corporate world.

195
00:09:39,052 --> 00:09:40,711
A partner named Anil Kumar.

196
00:09:40,778 --> 00:09:43,543
The government said he was paid, on the
side,

197
00:09:43,609 --> 00:09:47,542
for a private stream of the things he
learned while advising his

198
00:09:47,608 --> 00:09:51,786
clients. The entire promise of a firm like
that is discretion — that

199
00:09:51,853 --> 00:09:53,942
your secrets are safe in the room.

200
00:09:54,009 --> 00:09:57,265
That promise, prosecutors said, was
quietly for sale.

201
00:09:58,315 --> 00:10:00,178
The third sat high inside IBM.

202
00:10:00,245 --> 00:10:02,357
Robert Moffat, a senior executive,

203
00:10:02,424 --> 00:10:06,461
the kind of name that gets floated as a
future chief of the whole

204
00:10:06,528 --> 00:10:09,075
company. And the fourth, Danielle Chiesi,

205
00:10:09,141 --> 00:10:11,315
worked at a fund called New Castle,

206
00:10:11,382 --> 00:10:14,426
and traded in the same kind of secrets —
talking,

207
00:10:14,492 --> 00:10:18,530
the government said, in the careful
half-code of people who know,

208
00:10:18,597 --> 00:10:22,510
somewhere inside, that they should not be
saying this out loud.

209
00:10:23,560 --> 00:10:25,769
Look at that group for one second.

210
00:10:25,835 --> 00:10:28,239
An Intel insider. A McKinsey partner.

211
00:10:28,306 --> 00:10:30,449
An IBM executive. A fund manager.

212
00:10:30,516 --> 00:10:32,400
This was not some back alley.

213
00:10:32,466 --> 00:10:35,195
This was the boardroom, the corner office,

214
00:10:35,261 --> 00:10:36,885
and the consulting suite.

215
00:10:36,952 --> 00:10:41,434
The respectable rooms, the ones with the
good art and the city views.

216
00:10:41,501 --> 00:10:44,814
And inside those rooms, the government
would argue,

217
00:10:44,881 --> 00:10:49,428
the market that millions of ordinary
people trusted with their savings

218
00:10:49,495 --> 00:10:52,028
was being quietly, methodically robbed.

219
00:10:53,078 --> 00:10:55,929
Look again at exactly where these people
sat.

220
00:10:55,995 --> 00:10:57,325
A great chip company.

221
00:10:57,392 --> 00:10:59,166
A legendary consulting firm.

222
00:10:59,232 --> 00:11:01,133
A pillar of American business.

223
00:11:01,199 --> 00:11:03,733
Institutions that run entirely on trust,

224
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:08,234
on the simple belief that what happens
inside their walls stays inside

225
00:11:08,301 --> 00:11:12,608
their walls. The government's case was
that each of these places had

226
00:11:12,675 --> 00:11:15,905
been quietly hollowed out a little from
the inside,

227
00:11:15,972 --> 00:11:19,773
by someone they had trusted with their
most guarded secrets.

228
00:11:19,839 --> 00:11:22,627
The robbery here was not only of the
market.

229
00:11:22,693 --> 00:11:25,100
It was of the institutions themselves,

230
00:11:25,167 --> 00:11:28,651
betrayed by the very people they had
lifted to the top.

231
00:11:29,701 --> 00:11:32,634
Here is how a single secret turned into
money.

232
00:11:32,701 --> 00:11:36,208
An insider learns that a company is about
to be bought.

233
00:11:36,274 --> 00:11:37,677
He picks up the phone.

234
00:11:37,744 --> 00:11:41,123
Minutes later, a fund is buying that stock
— quietly,

235
00:11:41,190 --> 00:11:45,080
in pieces, spread out so that nothing
looks strange to anyone

236
00:11:45,146 --> 00:11:48,845
watching. Days later, the deal is
announced to the public.

237
00:11:48,911 --> 00:11:53,375
The price jumps. The position that was
bought on a whisper is suddenly

238
00:11:53,441 --> 00:11:55,546
worth millions more than it cost.

239
00:11:55,612 --> 00:11:58,354
Clean. Invisible. Worst of all,
repeatable.

240
00:11:59,404 --> 00:12:03,640
The quiet genius of a network like this is
that nobody inside it ever

241
00:12:03,707 --> 00:12:05,426
has to feel like a criminal.

242
00:12:05,492 --> 00:12:07,764
The insider is only helping a friend.

243
00:12:07,830 --> 00:12:09,611
The friend is only listening.

244
00:12:09,677 --> 00:12:11,826
The favor comes wrapped in dinners,

245
00:12:11,893 --> 00:12:14,594
in shared vacations, in years of real
trust,

246
00:12:14,660 --> 00:12:18,344
until the precise moment a secret crosses
the line is almost

247
00:12:18,410 --> 00:12:19,577
impossible to find.

248
00:12:19,644 --> 00:12:22,222
There is no mask. There is no getaway car.

249
00:12:22,289 --> 00:12:24,560
There is no single dramatic betrayal.

250
00:12:24,627 --> 00:12:27,205
There is just a relationship, slowly bent,

251
00:12:27,272 --> 00:12:29,482
one ordinary conversation at a time,

252
00:12:29,548 --> 00:12:33,723
until the bending quietly becomes the
whole point of the friendship.

253
00:12:34,773 --> 00:12:37,969
Here is the part that should genuinely
unsettle you.

254
00:12:38,035 --> 00:12:40,616
The people feeding those secrets were not,

255
00:12:40,683 --> 00:12:42,465
for the most part, desperate.

256
00:12:42,532 --> 00:12:44,621
They were not stealing to survive.

257
00:12:44,688 --> 00:12:47,515
They were already successful, already
wealthy,

258
00:12:47,581 --> 00:12:51,391
already admired. The temptation at the top
of the world is not

259
00:12:51,458 --> 00:12:53,056
survival. It is certainty.

260
00:12:53,122 --> 00:12:56,748
It is the chance to take the one thing the
market is never,

261
00:12:56,815 --> 00:12:59,027
ever supposed to sell, a sure thing,

262
00:12:59,094 --> 00:13:03,334
and to feel, just for a moment, like a
person who simply cannot lose.

263
00:13:04,384 --> 00:13:06,424
What did all of this actually earn?

264
00:13:06,490 --> 00:13:10,337
The honest answer is that it depends
entirely on how you count it.

265
00:13:10,404 --> 00:13:14,250
Different agencies used different methods
and arrived at different

266
00:13:14,317 --> 00:13:17,173
numbers, and we will come back to that gap
later,

267
00:13:17,239 --> 00:13:20,561
because the gap itself turns out to be
part of the story.

268
00:13:20,628 --> 00:13:24,474
But by any reasonable count, the trades
that ran on these whispers

269
00:13:24,541 --> 00:13:26,814
were worth tens of millions of dollars.

270
00:13:27,864 --> 00:13:31,208
Now remember the problem from the first
movement.

271
00:13:31,274 --> 00:13:35,505
Every one of these calls sounded, to
anyone listening from the

272
00:13:35,572 --> 00:13:38,369
outside, like two friends simply talking.

273
00:13:38,436 --> 00:13:39,869
Markets. The weather.

274
00:13:39,936 --> 00:13:41,846
Family. A name dropped here.

275
00:13:41,913 --> 00:13:43,619
A number mentioned there.

276
00:13:43,686 --> 00:13:48,257
Nothing a regulator could ever carry into
a courtroom and hand to a

277
00:13:48,324 --> 00:13:51,395
jury. The crime lived entirely in the
timing,

278
00:13:51,461 --> 00:13:55,078
and the timing was perfectly,
infuriatingly deniable.

279
00:13:56,128 --> 00:13:59,259
The man at the center of it understood
that completely.

280
00:13:59,326 --> 00:14:02,685
It is a large part of why people at his
level felt so safe.

281
00:14:02,752 --> 00:14:05,712
They were not hiding in some dark and
obvious place.

282
00:14:05,779 --> 00:14:09,708
They were hiding in plain sight, inside
conversations that looked and

283
00:14:09,774 --> 00:14:13,532
sounded completely ordinary, protected by
one simple and seemingly

284
00:14:13,599 --> 00:14:15,819
permanent fact. No one could hear them.

285
00:14:16,869 --> 00:14:18,254
No one could hear them.

286
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:21,150
That sentence is the hinge of the entire
story,

287
00:14:21,216 --> 00:14:25,069
so hold onto it. The crime was perfect for
exactly as long as it

288
00:14:25,136 --> 00:14:29,048
stayed unheard. Which meant there was
really only one way left to

289
00:14:29,115 --> 00:14:32,426
break it. Someone was going to have to
start listening.

290
00:14:33,476 --> 00:14:37,398
The investigators who finally moved on
Galleon came at it the slow

291
00:14:37,464 --> 00:14:38,712
way, the patient way.

292
00:14:38,779 --> 00:14:41,453
They started where these cases usually
start.

293
00:14:41,519 --> 00:14:45,382
With someone on the inside who agreed to
cooperate — a person who

294
00:14:45,448 --> 00:14:50,380
could tell them where to look, and confirm
what the trades had only ever hinted at.

295
00:14:51,430 --> 00:14:53,745
But a cooperator can always be doubted.

296
00:14:53,812 --> 00:14:57,313
A good defense lawyer can stand in front
of a jury and say,

297
00:14:57,380 --> 00:15:01,416
this person is a liar, this person is only
talking to save their own

298
00:15:01,483 --> 00:15:03,501
skin, do not believe a word of it.

299
00:15:03,568 --> 00:15:07,485
The investigators wanted something a
defense lawyer could not talk

300
00:15:07,552 --> 00:15:10,876
their way around. They wanted evidence
that could not be

301
00:15:10,942 --> 00:15:12,486
cross-examined into doubt.

302
00:15:12,552 --> 00:15:14,511
They wanted the calls themselves.

303
00:15:15,561 --> 00:15:18,431
So they did something that, in a financial
case,

304
00:15:18,498 --> 00:15:19,813
was almost unheard of.

305
00:15:19,880 --> 00:15:23,049
They went to a federal judge, laid out
what they had,

306
00:15:23,115 --> 00:15:24,550
and asked for a wiretap.

307
00:15:24,617 --> 00:15:28,563
Court permission to secretly record a
private citizen's telephone.

308
00:15:28,630 --> 00:15:32,756
For decades, that specific power had been
pointed at organized crime,

309
00:15:32,822 --> 00:15:37,008
at drug networks, at the kind of violence
that leaves actual bodies in

310
00:15:37,075 --> 00:15:41,200
the street. It had not been pointed at a
billionaire sitting behind a

311
00:15:41,267 --> 00:15:42,403
Bloomberg terminal.

312
00:15:44,303 --> 00:15:47,443
Understand how large a line that was to
cross.

313
00:15:47,510 --> 00:15:51,264
A wiretap is the most invasive tool the
government has.

314
00:15:51,331 --> 00:15:54,539
It does not capture one careful, chosen
moment.

315
00:15:54,605 --> 00:15:56,175
It captures everything.

316
00:15:56,242 --> 00:15:57,948
Every deal. Every secret.

317
00:15:58,015 --> 00:16:02,520
Every private, foolish, ordinary, human
thing a person says into a

318
00:16:02,587 --> 00:16:04,771
phone across the span of months.

319
00:16:04,838 --> 00:16:09,207
To aim that tool at Wall Street was to
declare something new and

320
00:16:09,273 --> 00:16:10,502
stunning out loud.

321
00:16:10,569 --> 00:16:14,118
The suits would now be treated exactly
like the mob.

322
00:16:15,168 --> 00:16:16,421
The judge said yes.

323
00:16:16,487 --> 00:16:19,059
And in 2008, on Raj Rajaratnam's phone,

324
00:16:19,125 --> 00:16:20,971
the recorders began to turn.

325
00:16:21,038 --> 00:16:25,258
For months, federal agents sat in a quiet
room and listened to a

326
00:16:25,324 --> 00:16:28,291
billionaire's life unfold one call at a
time.

327
00:16:28,358 --> 00:16:29,874
Most of it was nothing.

328
00:16:29,941 --> 00:16:31,523
Lunch plans. Complaints.

329
00:16:31,590 --> 00:16:34,227
The grind of an ordinary rich man's day.

330
00:16:34,294 --> 00:16:38,448
They waited, patiently, for the ordinary
chatter to harden into

331
00:16:38,514 --> 00:16:40,756
something a jury would understand.

332
00:16:41,806 --> 00:16:45,269
Consider the people in that listening room
for a moment.

333
00:16:45,335 --> 00:16:49,416
Night after night, headphones on,
transcribing a stranger's entire

334
00:16:49,483 --> 00:16:50,905
life. The dinner plans.

335
00:16:50,972 --> 00:16:54,063
The arguments. The long stretches of pure
boredom.

336
00:16:54,130 --> 00:16:56,356
Most of it meant absolutely nothing,

337
00:16:56,422 --> 00:17:00,071
and sitting through all of that nothing
was the actual job.

338
00:17:00,137 --> 00:17:02,610
They were waiting for a single sentence.

339
00:17:02,677 --> 00:17:06,758
The one moment when a careful man finally
forgot to be careful and

340
00:17:06,825 --> 00:17:10,473
said out loud the thing the trades had
only ever whispered.

341
00:17:10,539 --> 00:17:14,620
Patience, it turned out, was the one
weapon the men at the top had

342
00:17:14,687 --> 00:17:15,800
never planned for.

343
00:17:16,850 --> 00:17:17,945
Eventually, it did.

344
00:17:18,012 --> 00:17:21,469
Because here is the strange truth about
feeling untouchable.

345
00:17:21,536 --> 00:17:24,359
The longer it lasts, the less careful you
become.

346
00:17:24,426 --> 00:17:27,250
On a tapped line, the government would
later say,

347
00:17:27,316 --> 00:17:31,177
the scheme stopped being an abstract
pattern of trades and became a

348
00:17:31,244 --> 00:17:32,339
plain conversation.

349
00:17:32,406 --> 00:17:35,978
People discussed information they were
never supposed to have.

350
00:17:36,045 --> 00:17:38,811
They discussed it directly, in their own
voices,

351
00:17:38,878 --> 00:17:42,566
with their own names attached, into a
phone they were absolutely

352
00:17:42,632 --> 00:17:43,785
certain was private.

353
00:17:44,835 --> 00:17:48,090
That is the moment the whole world quietly
tilts.

354
00:17:48,157 --> 00:17:51,146
For the entire history of this kind of
crime,

355
00:17:51,213 --> 00:17:54,069
the secret had been invisible,
untouchable,

356
00:17:54,136 --> 00:17:56,261
impossible to hold in your hand.

357
00:17:56,328 --> 00:17:57,590
Now it was on tape.

358
00:17:57,657 --> 00:17:59,450
The whisper had a waveform.

359
00:17:59,517 --> 00:18:03,636
The deniable phone call had become a
recording — time-stamped,

360
00:18:03,702 --> 00:18:06,359
in the speaker's own unmistakable voice,

361
00:18:06,426 --> 00:18:09,681
sitting in a government evidence locker,
waiting.

362
00:18:10,731 --> 00:18:14,386
And you cannot tell a jury that a
recording is only guessing.

363
00:18:14,453 --> 00:18:16,669
You cannot say the tape was confused,

364
00:18:16,736 --> 00:18:20,511
or jealous, or just assembling a clever
mosaic of public facts.

365
00:18:20,578 --> 00:18:24,292
The defense that had protected the very
top of Wall Street for

366
00:18:24,359 --> 00:18:27,714
generations, the comfortable line that it
was only talk,

367
00:18:27,781 --> 00:18:31,795
only research, simply does not survive the
sound of your own voice,

368
00:18:31,862 --> 00:18:33,300
calmly saying otherwise.

369
00:18:34,350 --> 00:18:37,116
There is something uncomfortable in all of
this,

370
00:18:37,183 --> 00:18:40,179
and the honest version of the story has to
admit it.

371
00:18:40,246 --> 00:18:43,127
A government that can listen to a
billionaire can,

372
00:18:43,193 --> 00:18:44,980
in principle, listen to anyone.

373
00:18:45,046 --> 00:18:49,080
The very same tool that finally caught the
guilty could just as easily

374
00:18:49,147 --> 00:18:51,163
be turned to frighten the innocent.

375
00:18:51,230 --> 00:18:55,091
The question waiting at the end of this
case was never only whether

376
00:18:55,157 --> 00:18:56,310
one man had done it.

377
00:18:56,376 --> 00:19:00,295
It was whether a free country should hand
itself a power this large,

378
00:19:00,361 --> 00:19:04,222
and then trust that it would only ever be
pointed at the people who

379
00:19:04,289 --> 00:19:05,326
truly deserved it.

380
00:19:06,376 --> 00:19:08,520
On the morning of October sixteenth,

381
00:19:08,587 --> 00:19:11,803
2009, it ended the way these things end in
the movies,

382
00:19:11,870 --> 00:19:13,955
except that it was completely real.

383
00:19:14,022 --> 00:19:15,451
Agents came to the door.

384
00:19:15,518 --> 00:19:19,270
They arrested Raj Rajaratnam at his home
and walked him out — a

385
00:19:19,337 --> 00:19:22,315
billionaire in handcuffs, in front of the
cameras,

386
00:19:22,382 --> 00:19:23,573
on the morning news.

387
00:19:23,640 --> 00:19:27,452
That same day, federal prosecutors and the
SEC stepped up to the

388
00:19:27,519 --> 00:19:29,782
microphones and announced the charges.

389
00:19:30,832 --> 00:19:33,306
The image went around the world that
morning.

390
00:19:33,373 --> 00:19:37,166
A man who had spent his whole life as the
person everyone else called

391
00:19:37,233 --> 00:19:40,146
for answers was now the person being
walked to a car.

392
00:19:40,213 --> 00:19:43,732
For everyone who had ever quietly
suspected the game was rigged,

393
00:19:43,798 --> 00:19:46,932
and assumed nothing would ever actually be
done about it,

394
00:19:46,998 --> 00:19:50,627
that single picture said something simple
and almost unbelievable.

395
00:19:50,694 --> 00:19:54,377
Sometimes, the people at the very top do
get the knock on the door.

396
00:19:55,427 --> 00:19:59,008
Within days, the seven-billion-dollar fund
began to close down.

397
00:19:59,075 --> 00:20:01,179
The contacts stopped returning calls.

398
00:20:01,245 --> 00:20:04,997
The empire that had taken years to build
came apart in a matter of

399
00:20:05,064 --> 00:20:08,930
weeks. Because the one thing that had
truly held it all together had

400
00:20:08,996 --> 00:20:10,247
never been the trades.

401
00:20:10,314 --> 00:20:13,213
It had been the silence, and that silence
was over.

402
00:20:14,263 --> 00:20:16,540
But an arrest is not a conviction,

403
00:20:16,606 --> 00:20:18,749
and Raj Rajaratnam did not fold.

404
00:20:18,816 --> 00:20:22,834
He hired one of the finest defense teams
that money can buy,

405
00:20:22,900 --> 00:20:27,588
and then he made a choice that most people
in his position never make.

406
00:20:27,655 --> 00:20:29,463
He refused to plead guilty.

407
00:20:29,529 --> 00:20:32,543
He decided to take the whole thing to a
jury,

408
00:20:32,609 --> 00:20:36,694
and he bet — everything — that the tapes
would not be enough.

409
00:20:37,744 --> 00:20:40,250
The trial opened in Manhattan in 2011,

410
00:20:40,317 --> 00:20:43,483
and more than anything else, it turned on
sound.

411
00:20:43,550 --> 00:20:47,903
The prosecutors did not simply stand up
and describe the scheme in

412
00:20:47,970 --> 00:20:50,080
their own words. They played it.

413
00:20:50,147 --> 00:20:54,698
They sat the jury down, and they let
twelve ordinary people listen to

414
00:20:54,765 --> 00:20:57,469
the defendant's own recorded phone calls.

415
00:20:57,536 --> 00:20:59,976
Again, and again. Until the abstract,

416
00:21:00,043 --> 00:21:04,528
bloodless idea of insider trading became
something else entirely — a

417
00:21:04,595 --> 00:21:07,233
human voice, filling a silent courtroom.

418
00:21:09,133 --> 00:21:11,804
The defense did everything it possibly
could.

419
00:21:11,871 --> 00:21:14,483
It argued the trades were built on
research,

420
00:21:14,550 --> 00:21:18,646
on public information, on the ordinary
mosaic of facts that any sharp

421
00:21:18,713 --> 00:21:20,375
analyst assembles every day.

422
00:21:20,442 --> 00:21:23,766
For years, in case after case, that
argument had worked.

423
00:21:23,833 --> 00:21:27,395
It had been the great escape hatch at the
top of the market.

424
00:21:27,461 --> 00:21:30,489
But it is very, very hard to talk over a
recording.

425
00:21:30,556 --> 00:21:33,108
The jury did not have to imagine the
crime.

426
00:21:33,175 --> 00:21:34,303
They could hear it.

427
00:21:35,353 --> 00:21:38,117
The defense even had a name for its
argument.

428
00:21:38,184 --> 00:21:39,289
The mosaic theory.

429
00:21:39,356 --> 00:21:43,533
The idea that a brilliant analyst builds a
picture out of a thousand

430
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:45,934
small, public, perfectly legal pieces,

431
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,273
the way a mosaic is built from tiles,

432
00:21:48,340 --> 00:21:52,455
and that what looks from the outside like
a stolen secret is really

433
00:21:52,522 --> 00:21:54,303
just an unusually good guess.

434
00:21:54,370 --> 00:21:58,362
For years, that picture had been almost
impossible to take apart.

435
00:21:58,429 --> 00:22:01,500
It was the perfect defense for an
invisible crime.

436
00:22:01,567 --> 00:22:03,717
The trouble, this time, was simple.

437
00:22:03,783 --> 00:22:05,749
The tapes did not show a mosaic.

438
00:22:05,815 --> 00:22:07,351
They showed a phone call.

439
00:22:08,401 --> 00:22:11,178
On May eleventh, 2011, the jury came back.

440
00:22:11,245 --> 00:22:13,824
Guilty. And not on a few of the counts,

441
00:22:13,890 --> 00:22:15,742
as a kind of split decision.

442
00:22:15,808 --> 00:22:18,652
On all fourteen. Five counts of
conspiracy.

443
00:22:18,718 --> 00:22:20,834
Nine counts of securities fraud.

444
00:22:20,901 --> 00:22:25,464
The man that everyone in the business had
called untouchable had just

445
00:22:25,530 --> 00:22:29,961
been convicted, in open court, by twelve
completely ordinary people

446
00:22:30,027 --> 00:22:33,664
who had spent weeks doing nothing but
listening to him.

447
00:22:34,714 --> 00:22:36,988
When the foreman said the word guilty,

448
00:22:37,055 --> 00:22:40,884
fourteen times over, something cracked in
that room that was far

449
00:22:40,951 --> 00:22:42,148
bigger than one man.

450
00:22:42,214 --> 00:22:46,284
For a whole generation, the people at the
very top had operated on a

451
00:22:46,350 --> 00:22:47,846
kind of unspoken promise.

452
00:22:47,913 --> 00:22:50,127
That the rules quietly bent for them.

453
00:22:50,194 --> 00:22:53,904
That the worst thing that could ever
really happen was a fine,

454
00:22:53,970 --> 00:22:57,202
a settlement, an uncomfortable week in the
newspapers.

455
00:22:57,268 --> 00:23:01,278
A billionaire in a federal prison cell was
never part of that deal.

456
00:23:01,344 --> 00:23:03,917
The verdict tore the promise up, in
public,

457
00:23:03,984 --> 00:23:07,036
where everyone in every corner office
could see it.

458
00:23:08,086 --> 00:23:10,745
Five months later, on October thirteenth,

459
00:23:10,812 --> 00:23:12,693
2011, the sentence came down.

460
00:23:12,760 --> 00:23:14,771
Eleven years in federal prison.

461
00:23:14,837 --> 00:23:19,183
At the time, prosecutors called it the
longest prison sentence ever

462
00:23:19,250 --> 00:23:22,364
handed down in an American insider-trading
case.

463
00:23:22,430 --> 00:23:25,609
The number was not just a punishment for
one man.

464
00:23:25,675 --> 00:23:29,697
It was a message, fired down the entire
length of Wall Street,

465
00:23:29,764 --> 00:23:33,396
meant to be heard in every corner office
in the country.

466
00:23:34,446 --> 00:23:35,609
Then came the money.

467
00:23:35,676 --> 00:23:39,281
The court ordered him to forfeit more than
fifty-three million

468
00:23:39,348 --> 00:23:42,779
dollars, and to pay a ten-million-dollar
fine on top of it.

469
00:23:42,846 --> 00:23:46,684
And in a separate civil case, the SEC won
a record penalty of more

470
00:23:46,751 --> 00:23:50,531
than ninety-two million dollars, the
largest it had ever obtained

471
00:23:50,597 --> 00:23:53,796
against a single individual in an
insider-trading case.

472
00:23:53,862 --> 00:23:57,759
Add it all together, and the penalties
against him passed a hundred

473
00:23:57,825 --> 00:23:59,570
and fifty-six million dollars.

474
00:24:00,620 --> 00:24:03,869
Now, finally, come back to that gap in the
profit numbers.

475
00:24:03,936 --> 00:24:06,120
Depending on who was counting, and how,

476
00:24:06,187 --> 00:24:10,108
the illegal gains were put at anywhere
from twenty-odd million dollars

477
00:24:10,175 --> 00:24:12,920
to over sixty. We do not actually need one
clean,

478
00:24:12,986 --> 00:24:16,683
final figure, and you should be suspicious
of anyone who hands you

479
00:24:16,750 --> 00:24:20,503
one. The court's own number, the more than
fifty million dollars he

480
00:24:20,570 --> 00:24:24,211
was forced to give back, already says
everything it needs to say.

481
00:24:25,261 --> 00:24:27,338
The case did not stop with one man.

482
00:24:27,404 --> 00:24:31,558
Regulators eventually brought civil
charges against twenty-nine people

483
00:24:31,624 --> 00:24:34,532
and firms across the wider Galleon
investigation,

484
00:24:34,598 --> 00:24:38,752
and the broader sweep ended in dozens of
convictions and guilty pleas.

485
00:24:38,819 --> 00:24:40,895
The whisper network, it turned out,

486
00:24:40,962 --> 00:24:42,327
had a great many nodes.

487
00:24:42,393 --> 00:24:45,182
This was never the story of a single bad
apple.

488
00:24:45,249 --> 00:24:47,088
It was the story of an orchard.

489
00:24:48,138 --> 00:24:52,284
And one of those lines reached as high as
the case would ever go.

490
00:24:52,350 --> 00:24:55,539
In a completely separate trial, with its
own jury,

491
00:24:55,606 --> 00:25:00,071
a former Goldman Sachs board member named
Rajat Gupta was convicted in

492
00:25:00,137 --> 00:25:04,092
2012 of feeding Rajaratnam secrets from
inside one of the most

493
00:25:04,158 --> 00:25:05,944
powerful banks in the world.

494
00:25:06,011 --> 00:25:07,924
He was sentenced to two years.

495
00:25:07,991 --> 00:25:10,925
His was a different case, a different
verdict,

496
00:25:10,992 --> 00:25:14,244
a different punishment, but it answered
the oldest,

497
00:25:14,311 --> 00:25:16,862
most cynical question about Wall Street.

498
00:25:16,929 --> 00:25:20,692
Did the rot reach all the way to the very
top? Yes. It did.

499
00:25:21,742 --> 00:25:25,899
Rajaratnam's lawyers appealed, and they
aimed straight at the heart of

500
00:25:25,965 --> 00:25:27,331
the case. The wiretaps.

501
00:25:27,398 --> 00:25:29,654
If the recordings could be thrown out,

502
00:25:29,721 --> 00:25:33,105
then everything built upon them might
collapse with them.

503
00:25:33,172 --> 00:25:34,538
This was the real test.

504
00:25:34,604 --> 00:25:38,701
Not whether he had done it — the jury had
answered that — but whether

505
00:25:38,768 --> 00:25:42,687
the government had ever been allowed to
listen in the first place.

506
00:25:43,737 --> 00:25:46,192
In 2013, the appeals court gave its
answer.

507
00:25:46,259 --> 00:25:48,885
It upheld the wiretaps as lawfully
authorized.

508
00:25:48,952 --> 00:25:51,293
It ruled that the recordings could stand,

509
00:25:51,360 --> 00:25:53,130
and it affirmed the conviction.

510
00:25:53,197 --> 00:25:55,538
The method was now blessed by the courts.

511
00:25:55,604 --> 00:25:59,259
Listening to Wall Street the same way you
listen to the mob was,

512
00:25:59,325 --> 00:26:01,153
the judges said, entirely legal.

513
00:26:01,219 --> 00:26:04,417
A door that had been shut for a century
had been opened,

514
00:26:04,483 --> 00:26:06,539
and it was not going to close again.

515
00:26:07,589 --> 00:26:09,975
Raj Rajaratnam served most of his term.

516
00:26:10,042 --> 00:26:12,795
In 2019, he was released to home
confinement.

517
00:26:12,862 --> 00:26:14,942
He has never accepted the verdict.

518
00:26:15,009 --> 00:26:19,231
In a memoir, he still insists that he was
wronged — a research-driven

519
00:26:19,297 --> 00:26:22,968
trader, he says, caught in a net that was
cast far too wide.

520
00:26:23,035 --> 00:26:24,809
You are allowed to hear that.

521
00:26:24,876 --> 00:26:26,283
To be fair, you should.

522
00:26:26,350 --> 00:26:28,491
But the appeals court heard it too,

523
00:26:28,558 --> 00:26:32,780
and it disagreed, and the conviction
stands today exactly as the jury

524
00:26:32,847 --> 00:26:33,847
delivered it.

525
00:26:35,542 --> 00:26:38,008
So go back to the question we opened with.

526
00:26:38,075 --> 00:26:39,660
Not whether the rich cheat.

527
00:26:39,727 --> 00:26:41,077
You never doubted that.

528
00:26:41,144 --> 00:26:44,256
The question was what it actually takes to
catch one.

529
00:26:44,323 --> 00:26:45,732
Now you have the answer.

530
00:26:45,798 --> 00:26:49,380
It took the government deciding to treat a
billionaire like a

531
00:26:49,447 --> 00:26:53,440
gangster. It took a judge's signature
granting permission to listen.

532
00:26:53,506 --> 00:26:57,499
And in the end, it took the one piece of
evidence that even the most

533
00:26:57,566 --> 00:27:00,619
powerful man in the room cannot argue
with: himself.

534
00:27:01,669 --> 00:27:03,873
That is why this case still matters,

535
00:27:03,940 --> 00:27:05,287
all these years later.

536
00:27:05,353 --> 00:27:08,353
Not because one more fund manager went to
prison.

537
00:27:08,420 --> 00:27:12,277
But because of the day the most protected
people in the country

538
00:27:12,344 --> 00:27:14,793
learned that the line had quietly moved.

539
00:27:14,860 --> 00:27:18,472
That the old defense, the easy claim that
it was only talk,

540
00:27:18,539 --> 00:27:20,620
could now be recorded, and frozen,

541
00:27:20,687 --> 00:27:24,911
and played back in their own calm voice to
twelve strangers in a jury

542
00:27:24,978 --> 00:27:27,182
box. The whisper was no longer safe,

543
00:27:27,249 --> 00:27:29,208
and everyone at the top knew it.

544
00:27:30,258 --> 00:27:33,101
Most stories about Wall Street end with a
shrug,

545
00:27:33,167 --> 00:27:36,188
because most of them end with no one
really paying.

546
00:27:36,254 --> 00:27:38,505
A fine that costs less than the crime.

547
00:27:38,572 --> 00:27:41,118
A settlement with no admission of
anything.

548
00:27:41,185 --> 00:27:44,560
A famous name that is quietly back in
business by spring.

549
00:27:44,627 --> 00:27:48,773
This one ends differently, and that
difference is the entire reason to

550
00:27:48,839 --> 00:27:51,327
tell it. A man at the very top was caught,

551
00:27:51,393 --> 00:27:55,361
convicted, and sent to prison, on the
strength of nothing more than

552
00:27:55,428 --> 00:27:56,790
his own recorded voice.

553
00:27:56,857 --> 00:28:01,002
The system is not really built to do that
to its most powerful people.

554
00:28:01,069 --> 00:28:02,905
Once, against the odds, it did.

555
00:28:03,955 --> 00:28:07,668
The system does not catch the people at
the very top very often.

556
00:28:07,735 --> 00:28:10,635
This is the story of one of the times that
it did.

557
00:28:10,702 --> 00:28:14,357
If that matters to you — if you want more
stories about how the

558
00:28:14,424 --> 00:28:16,570
powerful are finally held to account,

559
00:28:16,637 --> 00:28:18,610
told straight, and told carefully,

560
